{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Better is the end of a thing than its beginning.</b> He says that the wise man should not love gifts; in every matter he does, he should look to what its end is, for that is the essence. Do you not see that the wise man takes medicine that is bitter at first because it benefits in the end, and refrains from eating something sweet that is good at its beginning and bad at its end, and that he should be long-tempered. Now 'erekh (long) is an adjective on the pattern of yeled, and the heh came as an addition."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:03.195083705Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}